<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Partition of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1793</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Second Partition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was the annexation of large parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1793. It followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a> and Russian intervention against the reformers. Accounts of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth treat it as a step towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a> of 1795, which ended the state entirely.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland.jpg" alt="Second Partition of Poland" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Mathiasrex Maciej Szczepańczyk, map based of layers of User:Halibutt translations of User:Alokasta · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Second_Partition_of_Poland_1793.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
